[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Generics, Type Erasure, and the PECS Principle in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java Generics provide compile-time type safety and eliminate the need for explicit type casting. However, understanding how they work under the hood and how to use them with wildcards is crucial for writing robust and reusable APIs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Generics and Type Erasure"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Type erasure is the mechanism by which Java implements generics. To maintain backward compatibility with older versions of Java that did not support generics, the compiler removes all generic type parameters during compilation and replaces them with their bounds (or Object if unbounded)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Box<T> {
    private T item;
    public void set(T item) { this.item = item; }
    public T get() { return item; }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"After type erasure, the bytecode for the Box class resembles the following non-generic implementation:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class Box {
    private Object item;
    public void set(Object item) { this.item = item; }
    public Object get() { return item; }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Wildcards and the PECS Principle"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Wildcards (?) represent an unknown type in generics. The PECS principle (Producer Extends, Consumer Super) is a memorable guideline created by Joshua Bloch in Effective Java to help developers determine whether to use an extends or super wildcard."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PE: Producer Extends - Use <? extends T> when your collection or data structure produces values (you only read from it)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"CS: Consumer Super - Use <? super T> when your collection or data structure consumes values (you only write to it)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Practical Example of PECS"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a method that copies elements from a source list to a destination list. The source produces items, while the destination consumes them."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src) {
    for (T item : src) {
        dest.add(item);
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, List<? extends T> acts as a producer because we read elements from it, and List<? super T> acts as a consumer because we write elements into it."}]
